            "concept": "A method in Java is a collection of statements that perform a specific operation and optionally return a value.\nKey characteristics:\n- **Signature**: Method name and parameter list (types and count).\n- **Return Type**: Any primitive (`int`, `boolean`, `double`), reference type (`String`, object), or `void` if nothing is returned.\n- **Pass-by-Value**: Java is strictly pass-by-value. When an argument is passed to a method, a copy of the primitive value or a copy of the object reference address is passed into the parameter.\n- **Static vs Instance**: `static` methods belong to the class and can be invoked directly (`Math.sqrt()`) without creating an object instance. Non-static methods require an object receiver.",
