            "id": "dbms-security-distributed",
            "title": "10. Database Security, SQL Injection Defense & Distributed DBs",
            "read_time": "12 min",
            "summary": "Harden relational systems using role-based access control (GRANT/REVOKE), parameterized statements, and explore CAP theorem and 2PC.",
            "concept": "Enterprise database architecture requires comprehensive security hardening and scalable distribution:\n1. **Database Security & Access Control**:\n   - Least Privilege principle: Grant only necessary table privileges (`SELECT`, `INSERT`) rather than superuser (`ALL PRIVILEGES`).\n   - SQL Injection Prevention: Never concatenate user inputs directly into SQL strings. Always use Prepared Statements (Parameterized Queries).\n2. **Distributed Database Systems**:\n   - **CAP Theorem**: A distributed system can guarantee at most two out of: **C**onsistency, **A**vailability, and **P**artition Tolerance.\n   - **Two-Phase Commit (2PC)**: Distributed consensus protocol ensuring all participating database nodes agree to commit or abort (Prepare Phase + Commit Phase).\n   - **Replication**: Primary-Replica (Leader-Follower) architectures for read scaling and high availability.",
            "syntax": "-- Role-based privilege grant\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT ON school_db.students TO 'ta_user'@'localhost';\nREVOKE DROP, ALTER ON school_db.students FROM 'ta_user'@'localhost';\n\n-- Prepared statement placeholder preventing SQL injection\nPREPARE student_query FROM 'SELECT * FROM students WHERE email = ?';\n",
